man I want to start with you because  about four years ago I was on this stage  with you and your friend John Krasinski  the two of you had co-written and  starred in promised land and my  understanding is that the germ of this  film comes from the two of you is that  true yeah John told had an idea about a  story about an uncle having to return  kind of to his hometown against his will  that he that he pitched me over dinner  and he knew I was looking for something  to direct so I really liked this idea  and I mean we really did not have much  at all  but but what we had we decided  immediately that Kenny would be our  favorite person to write it because he's  our favorite writer and so we we went  and pitched him this idea and he well  you guys don't really have a lot no but  like that scene you know and and then we  kind of we left it with Kenny and but  Kenny had a huge log jam of work and  other deadlines he had to hit four other  projects and so we were kind of at the  back of the line and and we went away  and in our own impatience kind of wrote  and produced and starred in promised  land and kind of forgot about this and  then Kenny came back around a couple  years later with with this draft that  was just beautiful but it was it was  long but everything that was in here was  in that draft and and that was when I  Chris Moore and I were on it on the  project and that was when we kind of  called each other and went oh my god  this is really special Kenny what about  the conversations that you had sparked  something in you what were you  interested in exploring from what they  talked about well I just really liked  the idea a lot I thought it was very  moving and interesting and the idea of  someone in that much trouble trying to  do something that big and coming up  against that big of an obstacle I  thought it was really interesting  Kimberly what about you what when you  first read it obviously I'm sure you  have people coming to you wanting help  wanting resources what caused you to  take a chance on this project um it was  really scripted then from there seeing  how much you know I got a chance to meet  Matt and then me Chris and seeing their  passionate behind the project and then  you know knowing that Casey Affleck or  somebody always wanted to work with it  just kind of all fell in line and we  were really you know wanting to get  behind her tours you know as our company  lunch so Kinney was a very special one  to start with so it feels really awesome  Lucas so many of us are discovering you  for the first time through the  performances Patrick which is so  terrific and so and brings such a great  ingredient my understanding is that of  the major cast members you were the last  in one of the last to come on board what  was the audition process like for you  and how excited did you allow yourself  to get as you continued the process yeah  so I auditioned five times I think six  times actually two for this movie and I  find that when I'm auditioning I III  often fantasize about what it would  obviously what it would be like to do  this movie and I picture what it would  be like to do these scenes and to like  to be if I mean it's kind of the dream  to like be a to be a part of a movie  with with these people like I with Casey  and Michelle and to like be a part of  this family and to to and so I found  myself fantasizing a lot over the course  of the auditioning process and then when  I got it it was like oh [ __ ] like okay  this isn't just a family I mean it is a  family but like there's actually like I  got a deuce I got to do something now  like I actually have to I got to produce  in the scenes that are just so intense  we're in a space of just a couple of  minutes your characters are going from  composed to the opposite of composed how  much time is necessary to reset before  you do it again well you don't have that  much time so you can't stand around and  say give me half an hour I'd like to  reset my countenance  and then Kenny's got his ideas of what  needs to be reset and changed and so  it's you which is good you know all of  those demands are always helpful no  matter how you they feel like  restrictions but they're usually things  that help you just not overthink things  ya know I mean I remember that that it  always when you're working on movies  that are this small there that goes so  quickly you never really have as much  time as you want but you've tend to  always have as much time as you need and  that day specifically maybe that last  scene is what you're talking about I  remember it kind of going by in a blur  it was like you know that was like one  of that was not the only scene to do  that day that was just one thing on that  day so there wasn't a lot of time in  between some effort was made to you know  press back your eyes and try and start  from the beginning but um no there  wasn't a lot of like space around it I'm  always intrigued Kenny by films that  portray family members and ones that do  it so well where the audience believes  wholeheartedly that these are people  that are with each other day in and day  out for decades how did you go about  trying to foster that family kind of  environment amongst your actors who did  play family members oh you know a lot of  its just staying out of the actors way  really because you know they come in  with so much and they have so many ideas  about who they are and what the  relationships are and and and there's  very respectful of the script and want  to call what's in the script and it's  always fun to look at actors scripts  when they're not looking because there's  all these notes like often anyway and  there's just that and just to hear them  start to talk about you just got a  little piece of everything that they put  into it so I I guess it's a question of  getting everyone oriented in the similar  way and just at least giving them my  version of the story and then having  that be a starting point my version plus  their version and then what happens when  they get in front of the camera is is  always a surprise and I'm almost always  a pleasant surprise and just there's a  five-year gap in the story and just  tracking specifically what happened in  those  five years and what didn't happen and so  you know when they get in the room  together they at least have the common  history to know and kind of you know the  kind of specifics that actors always  bring to the roles that it just astonish  you and you like oh my god he thought  about what he had to lunch that day and  he thought about like where he was when  he went yesterday and how many times  he's been there and you're like holy  [ __ ] he really worked this is a lot of  work but that's the results are that  they're really in the moment and in a  very very full way and that's seen  you're referring to indeed all the  scenes or that you know they just bring  this incredible I my recollection is it  was easy to reset because they're they  have so much they're carrying around  these these incredible burdens and so  all they have to do is see each other  and they start to just spill out of them  and it's amazing I mean it's one thing  to write that it's another thing to  embody it I don't know how they do it  well you guys all did a fantastic job  I congratulate this entire panel thank  you so much

